<figure class="visual">
        <figcaption class="visual-cap"><span class="label">Example · web application</span><h3>Route <code>POST /checkout</code> to the checkout service</h3><p>Choose ALB because the decision depends on the hostname and URL path. This example terminates client TLS at the ALB and opens a separate HTTPS connection to the target.</p></figcaption>
        <div class="service-lifecycle">
          <div class="stage-row"><article class="stage blue"><h4>1 · Client and network access</h4><p>DNS resolves the ALB name. The ALB security group allows TCP 443.</p><code class="stage-code">POST https://shop.example.com/checkout</code></article><article class="stage purple"><h4>2 · HTTPS listener :443</h4><p>Protocol and port accept the connection. ACM certificate and TLS policy apply; TLS terminates here. Optional mutual TLS can verify a client certificate.</p></article><article class="stage amber"><h4>3 · AWS WAF on this ALB</h4><p>A regional web ACL can allow, block, count, CAPTCHA, or challenge an HTTP request. A blocked request returns without calling an application target.</p></article></div>
          <div class="decision-split"><div class="rule-stack"><div class="detail-rule match"><b>Priority 10 · MATCHED</b><span>Host = shop.example.com AND path = /checkout*</span><span>forward → checkout-tg</span></div><div class="detail-rule"><b>Priority 20 · path = /static/*</b><span>forward → static-tg · skipped for this request</span></div><div class="detail-rule"><b>Default · no conditions</b><span>forward → storefront-tg · runs only when no custom rule matches</span></div></div><div class="detail-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div><div class="group-stack"><div class="stage green"><h4>4 · checkout-tg selects a target</h4><p>IP targets · HTTPS :8443 · HTTP/1.1 in this example. The target group’s configured algorithm chooses among eligible targets.</p></div><div class="node healthy"><span class="status healthy">✓ HEALTHY · SELECTED</span><b>10.0.21.18:8443 · AZ-a</b><small>separate backend TLS connection</small></div><div class="node unhealthy"><span class="status unhealthy">× UNHEALTHY · EXCLUDED</span><b>10.0.22.41:8443 · AZ-b</b><small>Target.Timeout</small></div></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="service-details"><article><h4>Listener and rule options</h4><p>HTTP can redirect to HTTPS. Conditions include host, path, header, method, query, and source IP. Actions include forward, redirect, fixed response, and supported authentication or JWT validation features.</p></article><article><h4>Target-group controls</h4><p>Targets can be instance, IP, or Lambda. Configure protocol version, routing algorithm, health checks, stickiness, slow start where supported, and deregistration delay.</p></article><article><h4>Network and operations</h4><p>Use subnets across at least two Availability Zones in a standard Region. Observe access logs, WAF logs, target health, latency, and ALB versus target-generated HTTP status metrics.</p></article></div>
        <div class="behavior-note"><strong>Behavior to keep in mind.</strong> If every registered target in a group is unhealthy, ALB fails open to those targets. Weighted forward actions do not automatically fail over from an empty or unhealthy target group. With an HTTPS backend, ALB encrypts the target connection but does not validate the target certificate.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Three distinct steps:</strong> the listener receives the request, the first matching rule selects an action and target group, and the group selects a healthy target.</div>
      </figure>
